nW1\n\nWINDOWS\n\n7'-0\"\n\nW1\n\n10'-0\"\n\n06-07\n\nSHEET TITLE:\n\nEXTERIOR ELEVATIONS\n\nSHEET NUMBER:\n\nA202\n\n2022 THINK ARCHITECTURE INC.\n\nPage 25 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":26,"text":"VINEYARD PLANNING COMMISSION STAFF REPORT\n\nMeeting Date: October 7, 2026\nAgenda Item: Senate Bill 284/City Code Compliance — Consideration of Ordinance No.\n\n2026-11\n\nDepartment: Planning\n\nPresenter:\n\nAnthony Fletcher\n\nBackground/Discussion:\nSenate Bill 284 (SB 284), adopted during the 2026 Utah General Session, substantially amended\nand reorganized state law governing municipal land use regulation. The legislation requires\nmunicipalities to update applicable land use ordinances, procedures, and application\nrequirements to comply with the new state standards.\nThe required changes affect several areas of the Vineyard City Code, including land use\nprocedures, Planning Commission responsibilities, subdivision review, development agreements,\nland use applications, accessory dwelling units, water-right exactions, enforcement, appeals, and\nrelated administrative requirements. A detailed summary and gap analysis are included with the\nattachments.\n\nPhased Implementation\nDue to the scope of SB 284, staff proposes implementing the required amendments through three\nordinances:\n1. Detached Accessory Dwelling Units (DADUs): Implements SB 284 requirements related to\nqualifying lots, existing accessory structures, parking, setbacks, design, occupancy, utilities, and\nrelated development standards.\n2. Remaining Land Use Provisions: Addresses the remaining SB 284 land use requirements,\nexcluding water-right exactions\n3. Water-Right Exactions: Addresses SB 284 requirements specific to water-right exactions and\nassociated procedures.\nEach ordinance will proceed through the applicable Planning Commission and City Council\nreview process as it is completed.\n\nPlanning Commission\nThe Planning Commission conducted the noticed public hearing and reviewed Ordinance No.\n2026-11 on September 2. Following public comment and discussion, the Commission continued\nthe item and directed staff to revise the ordinance based on comments received. Staff has revised\nOrdinance No. 2026-11 to reflect all comments received in the previous meeting and will be\nconsidered by the Planning Commission for formal action of making a recommendation to the\n\nCity Council.\n\nNext Steps\nStaff will forward the Planning Commission's recommendation and the revised ordinance to the\nCity Council for consideration. Staff will concurrently continue preparation of the remaining\nordinances necessary to complete the City's implementation of SB 284.\nPage 26 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":27,"text":"Fiscal Impact:\n\nRecommendation:\nStaff recommends that the Planning Commission forward a positive recommendation to the City\nCouncil for approval of Ordinance No. 2026-11 to bring the City Code into compliance with SB\n\n284.\n\nSample Motion:\n“I move that the Planning Commission recommend approval of Ordinance No. 2026-11 to the\n\nCity Council to bring the City Code into compliance with SB 284.”\n\nAttachments:\n\n1.\n\n10.01.2026 DADU Ordinance - Senate Bill 284\n\n2.\n\n10.01.2026 DADU Redline Ordinance - Senate Bill 284\n\nPage 27 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":28,"text":"DRAFT\n\nVINEYARD CITY, UTAH\n\nORDINANCE NO. 2026-11\n\nAN ORDINANCE OF THE CITY OF VINEYARD CITY, UTAH, AMENDING THE\n\nVINEYARD ZONING CODE SECTION 15.12.050 DISTRICT USE TABLE, SECTION\n\n15.30.010 PURPOSE, SECTION 15.32.280 CLEAR VIEW TRIANGLE ON CORNER\n\nLOTS, SECTION 15.34.060 ACCESSORY DWELLING UNITS, SECTION 15.38.030\n\nPARKING REQUIREMENTS, AND SECTION 15.60 DEFINITIONS, AND AMENDING\n\nSECTION 1.08.040(S) INTERSECTION DESIGN OF THE VINEYARD\n\nCONSTRUCTION AND DESIGN SPECIFICATIONS, TO CONFORM THE CITY’S\n\nACCESSORY DWELLING UNIT REGULATIONS TO UTAH CODE SECTIONS 10-20618 AND 10-21-304.\n\nWHEREAS, Vineyard is authorized to amend the City Zoning Ordinance pursuant to\n\nUtah Code Title 10, Chapter 20, Municipal Land Use, Development, and Management\n\nAct; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Utah Legislature enacted Senate Bill 284, Local Land and Water\nModifications, during the 2026 General Session, which the Governor signed on March\n\n18, 2026, and which enacts Utah Code Section 10-21-304, Detached accessory\ndwelling units, effective October 1, 2026; and\nWHEREAS, Vineyard City is a “specified municipality” as that term is defined in Utah\nCode Section 10-21-101, and is therefore subject to Utah Code Section 10-21-304; and\n\nWHEREAS, Utah Code Section 10-21-304(1)(a) requires a specified municipality to\nadopt a land use regulation that permits a detached accessory dwelling unit (DADU) on\nany lot or parcel that is 11,000 square feet or larger and contains a single-family\ndwelling, if the single-family dwelling is a permitted use on the lot or parcel; and\n\nWHEREAS, the City’s existing accessory dwelling unit (ADU) regulations at VZC\n15.34.060 require a minimum lot size of twelve thousand (12,000) square feet for a\ndetached accessory dwelling unit (DADU), contain no process for the conversion of a\nlegally constructed accessory structure to a DADU, and contain design and parking\nprovisions that require revision to conform to Utah Code Sections 10-21-304 and 10-20618; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Planning Commission held a public hearing on September 2, 2026\nand, after fully considering public comments and staff recommendations, recommended\napproval to the Vineyard City Council; and\n\nPage 28 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":29,"text":"DRAFT\n\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council, having reviewed the proposed text amendment,\nheld a public hearing on ______________, 2026; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council, having considered the recommendation of the\nPlanning Commission and the comments submitted by the public, has determined that it\nis in the best interest of the public health, safety, and welfare to adopt the proposed text\namendment to the zoning ordinance;\nNOW, THEREFORE, be it ordained by the City Council of Vineyard City, in the State of\n\nUtah, as follows:\nSection 1: Amendment of Section 15.60 “Definitions” of the Vineyard Zoning Code. The\ndefinition of “Dwelling, Accessory Unit” is amended, and the definitions of “Accessory\n\nDwelling Unit, Detached” and “Accessory Dwelling Unit, Internal” are enacted, as\nfollows:\n\nDwelling, Accessory Unit: An Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU) provides\ncomplete, independent living facilities with a separate dwelling entrance than the\nprinciple dwelling. An internal ADU is an attached dwelling unit to a single-family\nhome. A detached ADU (DADU) is a single-family dwelling unit which is located\non the same lot as a primary single-family home. A DADU is designed to be\nclearly subordinate to the principle dwelling.\n\nSection 2: Section 15.12.050 “District Use Table” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is\namended as follows:\n(1) In the District Use Table, under RESIDENTIAL, the use “Dwelling, SingleFamily” needs to be designated a Non-Permitted use in the Regional Mixed-Use\n(RMU) District.\n(2) Add the following items:\n\n5. Accessory Dwelling Units: In any zoning district established under VZC\n\n15.14, Special Purpose Zoning Districts, in which a single-family dwelling\nis a permitted use, a DADU is a permitted use on any lot or parcel that\nqualifies under VZC 15.34.060, notwithstanding the omission of that use\nfrom the district’s use table.\n\n6. As per UC Section 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not\nbe required for a DADU located in a zoning district that is primarily\nresidential.\nSection 3: Section 15.34.060 “Accessory Dwelling Units” of the Vineyard Zoning Code\nis amended as follows:\n\nPage 29 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":30,"text":"DRAFT\n\nPurpose: The purpose of the ADU ordinance is to promote areas in the city to\naccommodate an expanding population, establish regulations to address the health,\nsafety and welfare of the community, to provide flexibility for changes in household size\nassociated with life cycle and to enable a level of financial security for home owners and\nto implement Utah Code Section 10-21-304. It applies to both internal ADUs and\ndetached ADUs (DADUs).\n1. Location: Accessory dwelling units are allowed as an accessory use to a singlefamily detached residence and are limited to the following development\nconfigurations:\na. Within a detached accessory structures in accordance with section\n\n15.12.060 Dimensional Standards Table and Section 15.34.030\n\nAccessory Buildings.\nb. Within a single family home provided access to the accessory dwelling\nunit shall meet all applicable fire and building codes.\nc. Within prefabricated housing types intended for long-term placement that\nadhere to a permanent foundation and the designed in accordance with\nsubsection 4, External Appearance.\n\nAn ADU shall not be located:\na. Within a public utility easement or other recorded easement; or\nb. In a front-yard area of a lot or parcel.\n\nAccess to an ADU shall meet all applicable fire and building codes. A mobile\nhome, travel trailer, boat, or similar recreational vehicle shall not be used as an\n\nADU. See VZC 15.34.100, Parking Requirements Of Private Recreational\n\nVehicles In Residential Zoning Districts.\n\n2. Minimum lot size: A minimum lot size of eleven thousand (11,000) square\nfeet is required for lots containing a DADU.\n\n3. Detached accessory dwelling unit size: A DADU shall not exceed the\nsquare footage of the primary residence located on the same lot or parcel;\n\n4. External appearance: The architectural style, building materials and building\ncolors of an accessory dwelling unit shall be compatible and consistent with the\narchitectural style, materials, and color of the primary building.\n\n9. Utilities: An ADU shall be served by, the same water, sewer, electrical, and\ngas meters that serve the primary building. No separate utility lines, connections,\nor meters shall be allowed for an accessory dwelling unit.\n\nA DADU will not be allowed unless it has adequate inside access to sanitary\nsewer, culinary water, storm water, and electricity and there is sufficient capacity\nin each of these utilities.\n\nPage 30 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":31,"text":"DRAFT\n\nAll ADUs shall conform to the following standards:\na. An ADU shall contain complete cooking and bathroom facilities that are\nseparate from the facilities located in the primary dwelling.\ni. The cooking facility in the ADU shall contain:\n(1) A sink and water faucet;\n(2) Capacity for food refrigeration; and\n(3) A permanent, built-in stove top, range, or other similar\ndevice for cooking food.\nii. The bathroom facility in the ADU shall contain:\n(1) A sink and water faucet;\n(2) A toilet; and\n(3) A shower or bathtub.\n\n10. Parking: A single-family dwelling with an internal ADU shall provide a\nminimum of one onsite parking space additional to existing requirements for the\nprimary dwelling with a maximum of four (4) total parking spaces required for\nboth the primary home and ADU together.\n\nA single-family dwelling with a DADU that is less than 650 square feet shall\nprovide a minimum of one (1) additional onsite parking space. A single-family\ndwelling with a DADU that is greater than 650 square feet shall provide a\nminimum of two (2) additional onsite parking spaces.\n\nWhen a detached garage is converted into a DADU, any necessary parking\nspaces from that garage shall be replaced on-site.\n\nParking spaces shall be located on a paved surface or approved driveway. Only\ndriveways or parking pads containing a minimum length of eighteen feet (18’)\nmay be counted toward required onsite parking. The minimum length of a\ndriveway counting toward onsite parking shall not include any land associated\nwith street rights-of-way, sidewalks or public property/access. All onsite parking\nspaces shall contain a minimum width of eight feet (8’). Primary dwelling parking\nshall not be parked tandem with tenant parking and tenant parking shall not be\nparked tandem with primary dwelling parking. The area dedicated to onsite\nparking and driveway surfaces shall be limited in compliance with VZC Section\n\n15.38.030(2)(b).\n\n12. Entrance: Entrances and access to the accessory dwelling unit shall meet\napplicable building and fire codes. Only one (1) front entrance of the primary\nhome shall be located on the front of the primary dwelling.\n\n18. Conversion of Legal Accessory Building to a DADU: The owner of a\nlegally constructed accessory structure may convert that structure to a DADU\n\nPage 31 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":32,"text":"DRAFT\nsubject to the dwelling and accessory structure setback requirements applicable\nto the lot or parcel; and all applicable building, health, and fire codes.\n\n19. As per UC Section 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not be\nrequired for a DADU located in a zoning district that is primarily residential.\n20. Building Permit: A building permit is required to erect, construct, reconstruct,\nor alter an ADU, or to change the use of a building or structure to an ADU.\n\nSection 4: Section 15.30.010 “Purpose” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is amended as\nfollows:\nThe purpose of the issuance of a conditional use application is to allow the proper\nintegration of uses that may be suitable only if such uses are designed, arranged, or\nconducted in a particular manner. This chapter identifies and provides the procedures\nfor the review of conditional use applications (identified as “C” Uses) in the table of\nuses.\nAs per UC Section 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not be required for a\n\nDADU located in a zoning district that is primarily residential.\n\nSection 5: Section 15.32.280 “Clear View Triangle On Corner Lots” of the Vineyard\n\nZoning Code is amended as follows:\n\nWhere two local residential roads intersect within a planned subdivision, no view\nobstruction including a sight-obscuring fence, wall, sign, other similar structures, and\nlandscaping which exceeds two (2) feet in height shall be placed on any corner lot\nwithin a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the curb\nline thirty (30) feet from the projected intersection of such curb lines. Where no curb\nexists, the clear view area shall include that portion of the corner lot lying within a\ntriangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the property line\ntwenty (20) feet from the intersection of said property line. Use the Vineyard City Access\nManagement Manual to design sight triangle dimensions for all other intersections.\n\nPage 32 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":33,"text":"DRAFT\n\nSection 6: Section 1.08.040 (S) “Intersection Design” of the Construction and Design\n\nSpecifications is amended as follows:\n6. Sight Distance Triangle. Where two local residential roads intersect within a planned\nsubdivision, no view obstruction including a sight-obscuring fence, wall, sign, other\nsimilar structures, and landscaping which exceeds two (2) feet in height shall be placed\non any corner lot within a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines\nlocated at the curb line thirty (30) feet from the projected intersection of such curb lines.\nWhere no curb exists, the clear view area shall include that portion of the corner lot lying\nwithin a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the\nproperty line twenty (20) feet from the intersection of said property line. Use the\nVineyard City Access Management Manual to design sight triangle dimensions for all\nother intersections.\n\nPage 33 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":34,"text":"DRAFT\n\nSection 7: Section 15.38.030 “Parking Requirements” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is\namended as follows:\nOff-street Parking Requirements. The following minimum number of off-street, paved\nparking spaces shall be provided and maintained by ownership, easement and/or lease\nfor and during the life of the respective uses hereinafter set forth. Any proposed uses\nnot listed herein will be determined by the Planner through the site plan approval\nprocess.\n\nTable: Residential Uses — Parking Spaces Required\n\nSingle-family: 3.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nTwo-family: 4.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nThree-family: 4.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nMulti-family: Efficiency/Studio 1.0 Spaces/Unit; 1 Bedroom 1.5 Spaces/Unit; 2\n\nBedrooms 2.0 Spaces/Unit; 3 Bedrooms 3.0 Spaces/Unit. In addition to the required\nspaces, 0.5 guest stalls per unit shall be included.\n\nSection 8: Severability\n\nPage 34 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":35,"text":"DRAFT\nIf any section, subsection, sentence, clause, or phrase of this Ordinance is held to be\ninvalid or unconstitutional, such decision shall not affect the validity of the remaining\nportions of this Ordinance.\n\nSection 9: Effective Date\nThis Ordinance shall take effect on October 1, 2026, or upon passage and publication\nor posting as required by law, whichever occurs later.\n\nPASSED AND ADOPTED by the Vineyard City Council this _____ day of\n______________ 2026.\n\n___________________________________\n\nZack Stratton\n\nMayor, Vineyard City\n\nSEAL\n\nATTEST:\n\n___________________________________\n\nRobin Raines-Bond\n\nCity Recorder, Vineyard City\n\nPage 35 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":36,"text":"DRAFT\n\nJacob Holdaway\n\nDavid Lauret\n\nParker McCumber\n\nChristopher Price\n\nJacob Wood\n\nYES\n\nNO\n\nABSENT\n\nABSTAIN\n\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n\n_______\n_______\n_______\n_______\n_______\n\n________\n________\n________\n________\n________\n\nPage 36 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":37,"text":"DRAFT\n\nVINEYARD CITY, UTAH\n\nORDINANCE NO. 2026-11\n\nAN ORDINANCE OF THE CITY OF VINEYARD CITY, UTAH, AMENDING THE\n\nVINEYARD ZONING CODE SECTION 15.12.050 DISTRICT USE TABLE, SECTION\n\n15.30.010 PURPOSE, SECTION 15.32.280 CLEAR VIEW TRIANGLE ON CORNER\n\nLOTS, SECTION 15.34.060 ACCESSORY DWELLING UNITS, SECTION 15.38.030\n\nPARKING REQUIREMENTS, AND SECTION 15.60 DEFINITIONS, AND AMENDING\n\nSECTION 1.08.040(S) INTERSECTION DESIGN OF THE VINEYARD\n\nCONSTRUCTION AND DESIGN SPECIFICATIONS, TO CONFORM THE CITY’S\n\nACCESSORY DWELLING UNIT REGULATIONS TO UTAH CODE SECTIONS 10-20618 AND 10-21-304.\n\nWHEREAS, Vineyard is authorized to amend the City Zoning Ordinance pursuant to\n\nUtah Code Title 10, Chapter 20, Municipal Land Use, Development, and Management\n\nAct; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Utah Legislature enacted Senate Bill 284, Local Land and Water\nModifications, during the 2026 General Session, which the Governor signed on March\n\n18, 2026, and which enacts Utah Code Section 10-21-304, Detached accessory\ndwelling units, effective October 1, 2026; and\nWHEREAS, Vineyard City is a “specified municipality” as that term is defined in Utah\nCode Section 10-21-101, and is therefore subject to Utah Code Section 10-21-304; and\n\nWHEREAS, Utah Code Section 10-21-304(1)(a) requires a specified municipality to\nadopt a land use regulation that permits a detached accessory dwelling unit (DADU) on\nany lot or parcel that is 11,000 square feet or larger and contains a single-family\ndwelling, if the single-family dwelling is a permitted use on the lot or parcel; and\n\nWHEREAS, the City’s existing accessory dwelling unit (ADU) regulations at VZC\n15.34.060 require a minimum lot size of twelve thousand (12,000) square feet for a\ndetached accessory dwelling unit (DADU), contain no process for the conversion of a\nlegally constructed accessory structure to a DADU, and contain design and parking\nprovisions that require revision to conform to Utah Code Sections 10-21-304 and 10-20618; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Planning Commission held a public hearing on September 2, 2026\nand, after fully considering public comments and staff recommendations, recommended\napproval to the Vineyard City Council; and\n\nPage 37 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":38,"text":"DRAFT\n\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council, having reviewed the proposed text amendment,\nheld a public hearing on ______________, 2026; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council, having considered the recommendation of the\nPlanning Commission and the comments submitted by the public, has determined that it\nis in the best interest of the public health, safety, and welfare to adopt the proposed text\namendment to the zoning ordinance;\nNOW, THEREFORE, be it ordained by the City Council of Vineyard City, in the State of\n\nUtah, as follows:\nSection 1: Amendment of Section 15.60 “Definitions” of the Vineyard Zoning Code. The\ndefinition of “Dwelling, Accessory Unit” is amended, and the definitions of “Accessory\n\nDwelling Unit, Detached” and “Accessory Dwelling Unit, Internal” are enacted, as\nfollows:\n\nDwelling, Accessory Unit: An attached dwelling unit to a single-family home, or\nlocated above a detached garage serving a single-family home which is located\non the same lot as the single-family home designed to be clearly subordinate to\nthe principle dwelling. An accessory dwelling unit provides complete,\nindependent living facilities with a separate dwelling entrance than the principle\ndwelling.\n\nDwelling, Accessory Unit: An Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU) provides\ncomplete, independent living facilities with a separate dwelling entrance than the\nprinciple dwelling. An internal ADU is an attached dwelling unit to a single-family\nhome. A detached ADU (DADU) is a single-family dwelling unit which is located\non the same lot as a primary single-family home. A DADU is designed to be\nclearly subordinate to the principle dwelling.\n\nSection 2: Section 15.12.050 “District Use Table” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is\namended as follows:\n(1) In the District Use Table, under RESIDENTIAL, the use “Dwelling, SingleFamily” needs to be designated a Non-Permitted use in the Regional Mixed-Use\n(RMU) District.\n(2) Add the following items:\n\n5. Accessory Dwelling Units: In any zoning district established under VZC\n\n15.14, Special Purpose Zoning Districts, in which a single-family dwelling\nis a permitted use, a DADU is a permitted use on any lot or parcel that\nqualifies under VZC 15.34.060, notwithstanding the omission of that use\nfrom the district’s use table.\n\nPage 38 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":39,"text":"DRAFT\n\n6. As per UC Section 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not\nbe required for a DADU located in a zoning district that is primarily\nresidential.\nSection 3: Section 15.34.060 “Accessory Dwelling Units” of the Vineyard Zoning Code\nis amended as follows:\n\nPurpose: The purpose of the ADU ordinance is to promote areas in the city to\naccommodate an expanding population, establish regulations to address the health,\nsafety and welfare of the community, to provide flexibility for changes in household size\nassociated with life cycle and to enable a level of financial security for home owners and\nto implement Utah Code Section 10-21-304. It applies to both internal ADUs and\ndetached ADUs (DADUs).\n\n1. Location: Accessory dwelling units are allowed as an accessory use to a singlefamily detached residence and are limited to the following development\nconfigurations:\na. Within a Ddetached accessory structures in accordance with section\n\n15.12.060 Dimensional Standards Table and Section 15.34.030\n\nAccessory Buildings.\nb. Within a home single family home provided access to the accessory\ndwelling unit shall meet all applicable fire and building codes. Mobile\nhomes, travel trailers, boats, or similar recreational vehicles shall not be\nused as an accessory dwelling unit.\nc. Prefabricated Within prefabricated housing types intended for long-term\nplacement that adhere to a permanent foundation and the designed is in\naccordance with subsection 4, External Appearance.consistent with the\nprimary structure on the property.\n\nAn ADU shall not be located:\na. Within a public utility easement or other recorded easement; or\nb. In a front-yard area of a lot or parcel.\n\nAccess to an ADU shall meet all applicable fire and building codes. A mobile\nhome, travel trailer, boat, or similar recreational vehicle shall not be used as an\n\nADU. See VZC 15.34.100, Parking Requirements Of Private Recreational\n\nVehicles In Residential Zoning Districts.\n\n2. Minimum lot size: A minimum lot size of five thousand two hundred (5,200)\nsquare feet is required for homes containing an internal accessory dwelling unit.\n\nA minimum lot size of twelve eleven thousand (12,00011,000) square feet is\nrequired for lots containing a DADU.\n\nPage 39 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":40,"text":"DRAFT\n\n3. Detached accessory dwelling unit size: A DADU shall not exceed a square\nfootage of one thousand two hundred (1,200) square feetthe square footage of\nthe primary residence located on the same lot or parcel;Size. A detached\naccessory dwelling unit shall not exceed one thousand two hundred (1,200)\nsquare feet, and shall not be larger in size than the single-family dwelling located\non the same lot or parcel.\n\n4. External appearance: The accessory dwelling unit shall not alter the\nappearance of the structure as a single-family residence. There shall be no\nexternal evidence of occupancy by more than one (1) family, such as two (2)\nfront doors. The architectural style, building materials and building colors of an\naccessory dwelling unit shall be compatible and consistent with the architectural\nstyle, materials, and color of the primary building.\n\n9. Utilities: An ADU shall be served by, the same water, sewer, electrical, and\ngas meters that serve the primary building. No separate utility lines, connections,\nor meters shall be allowed for an accessory dwelling unit.\n\nA DADU will not be allowed unless it has adequate inside access to sanitary\nsewer, culinary water, storm water, and electricity and there is sufficient capacity\nin each of these utilities.\n\nAll ADUs shall conform to the following standards:\na. An ADU shall contain complete cooking and bathroom facilities that are\nseparate from the facilities located in the primary dwelling.\ni. The cooking facility in the ADU shall contain:\n(1) A sink and water faucet;\n(2) Capacity for food refrigeration; and\n(3) A permanent, built-in stove top, range, or other similar\ndevice for cooking food.\nii. The bathroom facility in the ADU shall contain:\n(1) A sink and water faucet;\n(2) A toilet; and\n(3) A shower or bathtub.\n\n10. Parking: A single-family dwelling with an internal ADU shall provide a\nminimum of onefour (4) onsite parking space additional to existing requirements\nfor the primary dwelling with a maximum of four (4) total parking spaces required\nfor both the primary home and ADU together.\n\nA single-family dwelling with a DADU that is less than 650 square feet shall\nprovide a minimum of one (1) additional onsite parking space. A single-family\ndwelling with a DADU that is greater than 650 square feet shall provide a\nminimum of two (2) additional onsite parking spaces.\n\nPage 40 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":41,"text":"DRAFT\n\nWhen a detached garage is converted into a DADU, any necessary parking\nspaces from that garage shall be replaced on-site.\n\nParking spaces shall be located on a paved surface or approved driveway. Only\ndriveways or parking pads containing a minimum length of eighteen feet (18’)\nmay be counted toward required onsite parking. The minimum length of a\ndriveway counting toward onsite parking shall not include any land associated\nwith street rights-of-way, sidewalks or public property/access. All onsite parking\nspaces shall contain a minimum width of eight feet (8’). Property ownerPrimary\ndwelling parking shall not be parked tandem with tenant parking and tenant\nparking shall not be parked tandem with owner primary dwelling parking. The\narea dedicated to onsite parking and driveway surfaces shall be limited in\ncompliance with VZC Section 15.38.030(2)(b).\n\n12. Entrance: Entrances and access to the accessory dwelling unit shall meet\napplicable building and fire codes. Only one (1) front entrance of the primary\nhome shall be located on the front of the primary dwellingvisible from the front\nyard.\n\n18. Conversion of Legal Accessory Building to a DADU: The owner of a\nlegally constructed accessory structure may convert that structure to a DADU\nsubject to the dwelling and accessory structure setback requirements applicable\nto the lot or parcel; and all applicable building, health, and fire codes.\n\n19. As per UC Section 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not be\nrequired for a DADU located in a zoning district that is primarily residential.\n20. Building Permit: A building permit is required to erect, construct, reconstruct,\nor alter an ADU, or to change the use of a building or structure to an ADU.\n\nSection 4: Section 15.30.010 “Purpose” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is amended as\nfollows:\nThe purpose of the issuance of a conditional use application is to allow the proper\nintegration of uses that may be suitable only if such uses are designed, arranged, or\nconducted in a particular manner. This chapter identifies and provides the procedures\nfor the review of conditional use applications (identified as “C” Uses) in the table of\nuses.\nAs per UC Section 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not be required for a\n\nDADU located in a zoning district that is primarily residential.\n\nSection 5: Section 15.32.280 “Clear View Triangle On Corner Lots” of the Vineyard\n\nZoning Code is amended as follows:\n\nPage 41 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":42,"text":"DRAFT\nIn all Zoning DistrictsWhere two local residential roads intersect within a planned\nsubdivision, no view obstruction including a sight-obscuring fence, wall, sign, other\nsimilar structures, and landscaping which exceeds two (2) feet in height shall be placed\non any corner lot within a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines\nlocated at the curb line thirty (30) feet from the projected intersection of such curb lines.\nWhere no curb exists, the clear view area shall include that portion of the corner lot lying\nwithin a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the\nproperty line twenty (20) feet from the intersection of said property line. Use the\nVineyard City Access Management Manual to design sight triangle dimensions for all\nother intersections.\n\nSection 6: Section 1.08.040 (S) “Intersection Design” of the Construction and Design\n\nSpecifications is amended as follows:\n6. Sight Distance Triangle. Where two local residential roads intersect within a planned\nsubdivision, no view obstruction including a sight-obscuring fence, wall, sign, other\nsimilar structures, and landscaping which exceeds two (2) feet in height shall be placed\non any corner lot within a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines\nlocated at the curb line thirty (30) feet from the projected intersection of such curb lines.\nWhere no curb exists, the clear view area shall include that portion of the corner lot lying\nwithin a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the\nproperty line twenty (20) feet from the intersection of said property line. Use the\nVineyard City Access Management Manual to design sight triangle dimensions for all\n\nPage 42 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":43,"text":"DRAFT\nother intersections.A clear line of sight must be provided at all intersections, see\n\nVineyard City Access Management Manual. The “Sight Distance Triangle” must be\ncalculated using the stopping “Sight Distance” of the road being intersected. The\nstopping “Sight Distance” is 200 feet for a Local street, 300 feet for a Collector and 425\nfeet for Arterial. The figure below illustrates the required “Sight Distance Triangle” based\non the “Sight Distance”.\n\nSection 7: Section 15.38.030 “Parking Requirements” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is\namended as follows:\nOff-street Parking Requirements. The following minimum number of off-street, paved\nparking spaces shall be provided and maintained by ownership, easement and/or lease\nfor and during the life of the respective uses hereinafter set forth. Any proposed uses\nnot listed herein will be determined by the Planner through the site plan approval\nprocess.\n\nTable: Residential Uses — Parking Spaces Required\n\nSingle-family: 4.03.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nTwo-family: 4.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nThree-family: 4.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nPage 43 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":44,"text":"DRAFT\n\nMulti-family: Efficiency/Studio 1.0 Spaces/Unit; 1 Bedroom 1.5 Spaces/Unit; 2\n\nBedrooms 2.0 Spaces/Unit; 3 Bedrooms 3.0 Spaces/Unit. In addition to the required\nspaces, 0.5 guest stalls per unit shall be included.\n\nSection 8: Severability\nIf any section, subsection, sentence, clause, or phrase of this Ordinance is held to be\ninvalid or unconstitutional, such decision shall not affect the validity of the remaining\nportions of this Ordinance.\n\nSection 9: Effective Date\nThis Ordinance shall take effect on October 1, 2026, or upon passage and publication\nor posting as required by law, whichever occurs later.\n\nPASSED AND ADOPTED by the Vineyard City Council this _____ day of\n______________ 2026.\n\n___________________________________\n\nZack Stratton\n\nMayor, Vineyard City\n\nSEAL\n\nATTEST:\n\n___________________________________\n\nRobin Raines-Bond\n\nCity Recorder, Vineyard City\n\nPage 44 of 45","ocr":false},{"page":45,"text":"DRAFT\n\nJacob Holdaway\n\nDavid Lauret\n\nParker McCumber\n\nChristopher Price\n\nJacob Wood\n\nYES\n\nNO\n\nABSENT\n\nABSTAIN\n\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n\n_______\n_______\n_______\n_______\n_______\n\n________\n________\n________\n________\n________\n\nPage 45 of 45","ocr":false}]